# Line endings are normalised here rather than left to each machine's
# core.autocrlf, so a checkout is byte-identical everywhere and a diff never
# shows a file as wholly rewritten because someone opened it on Windows.
#
# LF in the repository for everything; Git still checks out CRLF on Windows
# where a platform expects it, per the rules below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one deliberate exception. tools/create-shortcut.ps1 is run
# by powershell.exe — still Windows PowerShell 5.1 — and CRLF is that
# platform's native convention. The same file is already ASCII-only for a
# related reason: 5.1 reads a .ps1 as Windows-1252, so a UTF-8 em dash arrives
# as three garbage bytes and the parser dies pointing at the wrong character.
# The validator enforces the ASCII rule; this enforces the line endings.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
